  "upstream": [
    {
      "body": "nixos/modules/security/wrappers/wrapper.nix takes linuxHeaders as a buildInputs entry; the static stdenv writes nix-support/propagated-build-inputs into the output; the activation script copies the wrapper out of that package, so the file and its 7.20 MiB of kernel headers stay. The binary itself never references them. One postFixup = \"rm -rf $out/nix-support\" fixes it, and this costs every NixOS system with a setuid wrapper, not just small ones. Ledger #67.",
      "reference": "Ledger #67",
      "title": "Setuid wrappers propagate linuxHeaders into the runtime closure"
    },
    {
      "body": "nixpkgs calls systemd with util-linux = util-linuxMinimal to break it, and NixOS then writes that build into PID 1's PATH and the generator environment, so the image carries two util-linux builds. The lab removed the 8.72 MiB bin output by changing the two PATHs (#65); the remaining 2.47 MiB is referenced by the systemd binary and needs the cycle broken. Overriding the package instead is an infinite recursion, because systemdMinimal resolves systemd from the final package set.",
      "reference": "",
      "title": "The systemd / linux-pam / util-linux cycle forces a second util-linux"
    },
    {
      "body": "7.10 MiB against 1.80 for bashNonInteractive, which is already in every closure as runtimeShell. Pointing environment.binsh, users.defaultUserShell, environment.shells, the system path and amazon-ec2-utils at the non-interactive build still leaves two referrers, both hardcoded: stage 2's prepare-root shebang and networking.localCommands. Backlog e28.",
      "reference": "Backlog e28",
      "title": "pkgs.bash is bashInteractive"
    },
    {
      "body": "It charges two filesystem blocks per inode; with 13016 files that is 101.7 MiB of a 398 MiB estimate, which reads like the image is mostly slack. It is not: the VHD is sparse, so the estimate only has to be large enough. Halving the allowance moved the allocated size by 64 KiB (#71), and a 1 KiB block size ran the bootloader step out of space (#70). Worth a comment upstream so the next person does not spend three experiments on it. Two further items are blocked rather than upstream-shaped: a compressed store (e3) and any non-ext4 root (e7) both hit make-disk-image's assertion that a partition table implies ext4.",
      "reference": "",
      "title": "make-disk-image's automatic sizing cannot be tuned usefully, and its inode allowance is misleading"
    }
  ],
